"Look at the full Moon, and you will see the same dark patches your great-grandparents saw. In Chinese stories, they form a jade rabbit pounding medicine; in the West, people see a face, the Man in the Moon. Poets thousands of years ago saw exactly the same picture. The Moon always keeps one face toward Earth, and its other side stays hidden. Why? It is not because the Moon does not spin. The Moon does rotate, exactly once each time it goes around Earth, about every 27.3 days. Try it yourself: walk in a circle around a chair while always facing the chair. When you get back to where you started, you have also turned around once, facing every wall of the room. The Moon does the same dance, so its near side always faces us. Why are the two times exactly the same? The answer is tides. Earth's gravity stretches the Moon slightly, just as the Moon raises tides on Earth. Long ago, the young Moon spun faster. Earth kept tugging on its stretched shape like a brake, slowing the spin down until the bulge pointed straight at Earth and the Moon was locked in step. Most of the large moons in the solar system are locked to their planets in the same way. The lock is not perfect. The Moon's orbit is a slight oval, so it moves faster when it is closer to Earth and slower when it is farther away, while its spin stays steady. Its spin axis is also tilted a little. As a result, the Moon seems to rock from side to side and nod up and down every month, letting us peek around its edges. Over time, we can see about 59 percent of its surface. No one saw the far side until 1959, when the Soviet spacecraft Luna 3 photographed it. People sometimes call it the dark side, but it gets just as much sunlight as the near side; at new Moon, the far side is fully lit. In 2019, China's Chang'e 4 made the first landing on the far side and sent out a rover named Yutu-2, or Jade Rabbit 2. In 2024, Chang'e 6 brought back the first rocks from the far side. The same face has looked down on all of human history. More than 2,000 years ago, the poet Qu Yuan asked why a rabbit sits in the Moon's belly. Tides are still at work today: they push the Moon about 3.8 centimeters farther away each year and slow down Earth's spin, making our day longer by about two thousandths of a second every hundred years. Look below to see how the real Moon is tipped today, and watch it rock over the coming weeks.": "e18b383485a7",
"No one saw the far side until 1959, when the Soviet spacecraft Luna 3 photographed it. People sometimes call it the dark side, but it gets just as much sunlight as the near side; at new Moon, the far side is fully lit. In 2019, China's Chang'e 4 made the first landing on the far side and sent out a rover named Yutu-2, or Jade Rabbit 2. In 2024, Chang'e 6 brought back the first rocks from the far side.": "48813830a09a",
